From dd3b53bfd8ba25a086a67eda4443e3b61e9c5446 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Hodgson <tom@justsanity.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 21:55:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] deps: make spirv-tools depend on the Python it is pointed
 at

SPIRV_TOOLS_EXTRA_ARGS sets Python3_EXECUTABLE to ${PYTHON_BINARY},
the interpreter built by external_python, but external_spirv_tools only
declared a dependency on external_spirv_headers. A parallel build can
therefore configure spirv-tools before Python has finished installing:

  Could NOT find Python3 (missing: Interpreter)
    Cannot run the interpreter ".../python/bin/python3.13"

Reproduced on both an Apple Silicon cross-compile and a native Intel
runner, so it is a build-graph race rather than anything architecture
specific. external_shaderc already declares external_python for exactly
the same reason; this brings spirv-tools in line.
---
 build_files/build_environment/cmake/vulkan.cmake | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/build_files/build_environment/cmake/vulkan.cmake b/build_files/build_environment/cmake/vulkan.cmake
index 90ae0d19087..d676f577e5e 100644
--- a/build_files/build_environment/cmake/vulkan.cmake
+++ b/build_files/build_environment/cmake/vulkan.cmake
@@ -82,6 +82,15 @@ ExternalProject_Add(external_spirv_tools
 add_dependencies(
   external_spirv_tools
   external_spirv_headers
+  # SPIRV_TOOLS_EXTRA_ARGS points Python3_EXECUTABLE at the Python we build
+  # ourselves, so that build has to finish first. Without this edge a parallel
+  # build can configure spirv-tools while Python is still being installed:
+  #
+  #   Could NOT find Python3 (missing: Interpreter)
+  #     Cannot run the interpreter ".../python/bin/python3.13"
+  #
+  # external_shaderc already declares this for the same reason.
+  external_python
 )
 
 if(UNIX AND NOT APPLE)
